        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Cannae</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">2 August 216 BCE according to Macrobius, citing the annalist Quadrigarius; P. S. Derow identified the Julian equivalent as 1 July 216 BCE, and others have suggested other dates</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Near the ancient village of Cannae on the left bank of the river Aufidus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and its Italian allies under the consuls Lucius Aemilius Paullus and Gaius Terentius Varro, with approximately 86,000 men by a general estimate; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and its allies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, perhaps 40,000 to 50,000, a figure to be treated with caution</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">Most of the Roman army was surrounded and killed; ancient and modern figures for the dead range widely. Capua and several other Italian cities went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> refused to make peace.</span></div>
        </div>
