    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ancient and modern figures for Roman dead range from 10,500 to 16,000 (Cantalupi) to 70,000 (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>), and figures for ancient battles, Carthaginian ones above all, are often unreliable.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s centre gave ground step by step while his African infantry and cavalry closed round the Romans, who were packed so tightly that they could hardly use their weapons.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> did not accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s offer to ransom prisoners or to make peace, raised new legions and fought on for 14 more years until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zama">Battle of Zama</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Within three campaign seasons, about 20 months, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> is said to have lost one fifth, some 150,000, of its male citizens over 17, and it buried people alive as a sacrifice after the defeat.</p></aside>
    </section>

