    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">218 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and defeats the Romans at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">217 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> defeats the Romans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fabius-maximus">Quintus Fabius Maximus</a> is made dictator and avoids pitched battle</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">216 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Varro and Paullus are elected consuls and given a newly raised army of unprecedented size</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Spring 216 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> seizes the supply depot at Cannae and the consuls march south to meet him</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">216 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s army encircles and destroys most of the Roman army at Cannae</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">216 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Capua and other Italian cities defect to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> rejects his offer to ransom prisoners</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">211 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> first attacks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, five years after Cannae</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-cannae" data-v="2bca6e19d5" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies draw up facing each other</b> The Romans faced east with the Aufidus on their right and massed their infantry deep in the centre. Hannibal put the Hispanic and Gallic infantry in the middle, the African infantry at the ends of the infantry line and the cavalry on the flanks.</li><li><b>The cavalry clash and the centres advance</b> The battle opened with a fierce cavalry fight on both flanks. In the centre Hannibal moved the Hispanic and Gallic infantry forward in a curve, keeping the Africans back.</li><li><b>The Roman cavalry is driven off</b> The Carthaginian horse got the upper hand on the Roman right. Hasdrubal did not chase them but rode to the other side of the field, and the allied cavalry broke between him and the Numidians.</li><li><b>The centre gives way step by step</b> The Roman heavy infantry pressed forward and Hannibal held his men together in a controlled retreat. The curve buckled inwards as the Romans advanced and reserve lines pushed into the gaps.</li><li><b>The Africans turn in and the cavalry takes the rear</b> The Carthaginian cavalry attacked the Roman centre from behind. Hannibal then ordered the African infantry to turn inwards against the Roman flanks, which formed a pincer.</li><li><b>The Romans are enclosed and killed</b> Enclosed in a pocket with no means of escape, the Roman infantry was cut down by the cavalry behind it, the African infantry on both sides and the centre in front.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hannibal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hannibal.jpg" alt="Hannibal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hannibal_Barca_bust_from_Capua_photo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fratelli Alinari, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> in the summer and early autumn of 218 BCE and won major victories at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a>. The Romans then appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fabius-maximus">Quintus Fabius Maximus</a> Verrucosus dictator. Fabius used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> warfare, cutting Hannibal's supply lines and avoiding pitched battle, which grew unpopular as the Romans recovered from the shock. Most wanted a quick end to the war, and it was feared that if Hannibal went on plundering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> unopposed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s allies might defect for their own safety.</p><p>The Senate did not renew Fabius's powers, and command passed to the consuls Gnaeus Servilius Geminus and Marcus Atilius Regulus. In 216 BCE Gaius Terentius Varro and Lucius Aemilius Paullus were elected consuls, given a newly raised army of unprecedented size and told to engage Hannibal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> writes that most wars were decided by one consul and two legions with their allies, but that in the alarm of that year Rome resolved to bring in eight legions, each of 5,000 men besides allies.</p><p>Ordinarily each consul commanded his own part of the army, but since the armies were combined, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> required the consuls to alternate command daily. In the spring of 216 BCE Hannibal seized the large supply depot at Cannae in the Apulian plain, placing himself between the Romans and a crucial source of supply. The consuls marched south and after two days found him on the left bank of the Aufidus, and camped 8 kilometres away.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Cannae&#8221; cites where it covers Strategic background, Prelude:</p><ul><li>Gregory Daly, Cannae: The Experience of Battle in the Second Punic War (Routledge, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gauls"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gauls.jpg" alt="Gauls" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dying_gaul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">antmoose, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Estimates of the Roman strength vary. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> normally used four legions of 4,000 foot and 200 horse; for the first time, according to contemporary Roman sources, eight legions of 5,000 foot and 300 horse were fielded, with allies of the same infantry strength and 900 horse for each legion. The eight legions form a core of some 40,000 men and an estimated 2,400 cavalry, and a general estimate is about 86,000 Roman and allied troops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> quotes one source saying the Romans added only 10,000 men to their usual army.</p><p>The Carthaginian army may have numbered between 40,000 and 50,000, a figure to be treated with caution, as are all those for the Carthaginian side. Its infantry is estimated at 8,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a>, 5,500 Gaetulians, 16,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> (8,000 of them left in camp on the day) and 8,000 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. The cavalry was 4,000 Numidian, 2,000 Iberian, 4,000 Gallic and 450 Libyan-Phoenician, with 1,000 to 2,000 Balearic slingers and 6,000 javelin men. The troops were united by their personal tie to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>.</p><p>The Romans drew up in the usual order, infantry in the centre and cavalry on the wings, but deeper than usual to break through Hannibal's centre as they had done at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a>. The centre was commanded by Geminus Servilius, Varro had the left and Paullus the right. Hannibal placed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a>, Celtiberians and Gauls in the middle, alternating them, with himself and his brother Mago, and the African infantry on the wings. Hasdrubal led the Hispanic and Gallic cavalry on the left by the Aufidus, which protected that flank, and Hanno the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numidians">Numidians</a> on the right.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Cannae&#8221; cites where it covers Armies, Tactical deployment:</p><ul><li>Gregory Daly, Cannae: The Experience of Battle in the Second Punic War (Routledge, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The armies had stayed in their camps for two days. On 1 August <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> offered battle but Paullus refused, and Hannibal's cavalry then harassed the Romans' water-bearers. Paullus opposed fighting on open ground, where Hannibal held the cavalry advantage, but thought withdrawal unwise. On the morning of the battle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> had manoeuvred so that the Romans faced east, with the low sun and dust blown by the wind in their eyes. Appian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> say Hannibal sent 500 to 600 men to feign desertion and attack the camp from within, though this is disputed.</p><p>Hannibal advanced the centre of his line in a crescent curving towards the Romans. The battle began with a fierce cavalry fight on the wings. The Hispanic and Gallic horse won on the Roman right, and Hasdrubal led them round to the other side to attack the allied cavalry still fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numidians">Numidians</a>, which broke. As the Roman heavy infantry attacked, Hannibal stood in the weak centre and held it in a controlled retreat, so that the crescent buckled inwards. The Romans, pressing forward, were packed so closely that they had little room for their weapons, and had ignored the African troops on the projecting ends.</p><p>The Carthaginian cavalry drove off the Roman horse and attacked the Roman rear. Hannibal ordered the African infantry to turn inwards on the Roman flanks, and the Romans were enclosed in a pocket with no way out. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> says that as the outer ranks were cut down the survivors huddled together and were killed where they stood. Livy describes wounded men overpowered by the enemy as they rose from among the dead.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Cannae&#8221; cites where it covers Battle:</p><ul><li>Mark Healy, Cannae 216 BC: Hannibal smashes Rome's Army (Osprey, 1994)</li><li>Gregory Daly, Cannae: The Experience of Battle in the Second Punic War (Routledge, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Philip V of Macedon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Philip_V_of_Macedon_in_Palazzo_Massimo_(Rome).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Livioandronico2013, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A few Romans escaped. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> agree that Varro got away to Venusia with 70 horsemen. Polybius says perhaps 3,000 infantry and 300 allied horsemen escaped. Livy names 600 men under Publius Sempronius Tuditanus who fought out of the smaller camp, and 4,000 infantry and 200 horsemen from the larger camp who reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canusium">Canusium</a>, and says Varro reported assembling 10,000 survivors. Only about 15,000 Romans are said to have survived, most from the garrisons of the camps.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was in panic. The authorities consulted the Sibylline Books, sent Quintus Fabius Pictor to the oracle at Delphi, and buried people alive as a sacrifice. They lowered the draft age and enlisted criminals, debtors and slaves to raise two new legions. Capua, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a>, Arpi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salapia">Salapia</a> and other southern cities went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>'s Greek cities rose in revolt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> pledged support, and Hieronymus of Syracuse made an alliance the next year.</p><p>Hannibal's offer to ransom survivors was rejected, and his delegation under Carthalo was refused a hearing on moderate terms. The Senate declared full mobilisation, prohibited the word peace and limited mourning to 30 days. In the story told, the Numidian commander Maharbal urged Hannibal to march on Rome and said that Hannibal knew how to win a victory but not how to use it. Hans Delbrück argued that Hannibal's army, after its losses, could not assault the city; Field Marshal Montgomery believed Maharbal was right. Rome fought 14 more years until Zama.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Cannae&#8221; cites where it covers Roman, Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Hans Delbrück, History of the Art of War volume 1: Warfare in Antiquity (University of Nebraska Press, 1975)</li><li>Gregory Daly, Cannae: The Experience of Battle in the Second Punic War (Routledge, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/polybius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polybius.jpg" alt="Polybius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_des_Polybios.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jona Lendering, Livius Onderwijs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>There are three main accounts of the battle, none contemporary. The nearest is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, who wrote about 50 years after it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> wrote under Augustus and Appian later still. Polybius presents the battle as the lowest point of Roman fortunes, perhaps to stress the later recovery; some say his casualty figures are exaggerated, 'more symbolic than factual'. Livy presents the Senate in heroic terms and puts the blame on the low-born Varro, clearing the soldiers he idealises. Appian's account is unrelated to Livy's and Polybius's, and scholars tend to discount it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> gives details, without a connected narrative, in his lives of Fabius and Aemilius Paulus. The date is uncertain, as the pre-Julian Roman calendar did not match the Julian; Macrobius gives 2 August, and Derow identified the Julian date as 1 July. The casualty figures differ widely: Polybius has 70,000 dead and 10,000 captured; Appian and Plutarch 50,000, and Quintilian 60,000. Cantalupi proposed 10,500 to 16,000, and Samuels and Clodfelter also doubt the larger figures.</p><p>Who commanded on the day is also in question. The traditional account puts Varro in command and gives him the blame, but Martin Samuels doubts it, because the Senate's warm reception of Varro contrasted with its treatment of other defeated commanders. Gregory Daly notes the Roman right was the place of command, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> at Zama claimed to have fought Paullus, so it is impossible to be sure. Some historians call Polybius's account of the crescent fanciful and see a natural bending of the line.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Cannae&#8221; cites where it covers Historical sources:</p><ul><li>Gregory Daly, Cannae: The Experience of Battle in the Second Punic War (Routledge, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus.jpg" alt="Scipio Africanus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Sulla_(loan_from_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek)_-_Glyptothek_-_Munich_-_Germany_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Luiz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cannae is one of the worst defeats in Roman history. Adrian Goldsworthy compares its death toll to the first day of the Somme in 1916. It led to reforms: the Roman infantry, which had fought in a mass resembling a Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>, was gradually articulated into small tactical bodies, as at Ilipa and Zama, leading in time to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohort</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a>. Unified command came to be seen as necessary, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> was made general-in-chief in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The battle also exposed the limits of a citizen militia, and the army gradually became a professional force.</p><p>The battle became a model. It is called the archetypal battle of annihilation and the first successful pincer movement recorded in detail in the Western world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a> wrote that every ground commander seeks a battle of annihilation and tries to repeat the example of Cannae. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> and Helmuth von Moltke tried to create their own Cannae, and Hans Delbrück's study influenced Alfred von Schlieffen, whose plan was inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s double envelopment. In 1991 Norman Schwarzkopf named Cannae as an inspiration for coalition operations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-war">Gulf War</a>.</p><p>Some writers question the lesson. Robert O'Connell argues Hannibal's only chance of winning the war was to march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Others infer from Hannibal's conduct, and from the fact that he first attacked Rome only in 211 BCE, that his aim was to wear the Romans down to a moderate peace by stripping them of allies.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Cannae on 2 August 216 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s army surrounded a larger Roman and allied force under the consuls Paullus and Varro and killed most of it, though the Roman dead are put at anything from about 10,000 to 70,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> refused terms and went on to win the war at Zama.</em></p>

